Criteria come from the document, in a fixed order:
  ok    seven requirements found (got 7)
  ok    qualifications first
  ok    nothing is weighted until somebody weights it — no defaults anywhere
  ok    and a vacancy nobody has weighted has no score, rather than a zero

A weight somebody set replaces the default:
  ok    English is worth 10
  ok    Python is worth 5
  ok    a requirement nobody weighted stays at zero

The sum:
  ok    everything met is 100
  ok    nothing evidenced is 0
  ok    half of two equal requirements is 50
  ok    a partial match is worth half

Weight is what makes one requirement matter more than another:
  ok    the heavy one met and the light one missing is 90
  ok    the other way round is 10

The edges:
  ok    no score at all when every weight is zero, rather than a 0 that reads as a bad candidate
  ok    a requirement weighted 0 is still judged and still counts for nothing
  ok    a criterion the model said nothing about counts as not evidenced, never as skipped
  ok    a reworded requirement cannot be matched back, and does not silently score

Requirements HR added:
  ok    an added duplicate of a stated requirement is dropped
  ok    and the stated one is the one that survives
  ok    a genuinely new requirement is added, marked as added
  ok    added requirements come after the ones the document states
  ok    weights adding to 100 make each weight its own percentage

The hiring record, as a second set of weights:
  ok    a requirement every hire evidenced carries full weight in the second figure
  ok    and one none of them evidenced carries none
  ok    by the weights that were set, this candidate scores 10
  ok    by the hiring record, the same verdicts score 100
  ok    and none of it is shown below three hires, where the pattern is one person

the scorecard adds up
